{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/periodic-field-cross-demo-test.md", "content": "# Periodic-field hypothesis test across heterogeneous demos

When a demo output is a dense repeating field but other examples look unlike it, neither force a shared rule from visual similarity nor dismiss the periodic case as unrelated. Treat the pattern as a testable abstraction: record the output's row/column period, color cycle ordering, direction, and phase/origin; compare these with the input's sparse diagonal tracks, marker sequence, or stripe ordering. Distinguish period from phase (a correct cycle with wrong origin still fails exact replay).

Replay the proposed generator against every complete demo. Include cases where the output is uniform or striped: these may be degenerate instances of a shared periodic generator, or evidence against it, and must not be ignored. A test input that visually resembles a periodic encoding only motivates the hypothesis; it does not establish mapping. If no deterministic cycle/phase rule reproduces all outputs, label it unresolved rather than submitting cosmetic rotations/reversals or repeatedly requesting demos without a discriminating prediction.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-4/play/users/u012/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/periodic-field-cross-demo-test.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/session-task-4f5f6fe0-ray-extension.md", "content": "# Diagonal boundary-ray extension

In this instance, the initial sparse motif contained two diagonal strands. The first demo showed the existing motif unchanged and added color-2 cells continuing diagonal paths outward from the motif's two exposed endpoints, reaching grid boundaries. A second, larger demo confirmed color-9 diagonal rays launched outward from the lower endpoints of a compact motif, with the motif retained and rays continuing to the bottom/right boundary. The test's motif uses color 6 for these rays. The output was the original 14×25 grid plus color-6 cells at (r, 13-r) for r=5…13, preserving all original cells.

Reusable inference: use cellwise deltas to identify ray sources, not all motif pixels; confirm direction and color on more than one demo; preserve unchanged motif cells and extend to the boundary. Validate the complete predicted output before submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-4/play/users/u012/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/session-task-4f5f6fe0-ray-extension.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/template-stamp-marker-separation.md", "content": "# Separating markers from a stamp template

For a grid with scattered single-color marks and a distinctive multicolor shape, map the output delta against the input before deciding the shape's role. Record each copied footprint's exact coordinates and colors, then compare those to both scattered markers and any instance embedded in the source template. The template's own center may or may not be an active marker; infer this from the demo, rather than assuming all similar-colored cells are equivalent.

Define one explicit anchor and offsets for every template cell, including vertical/asymmetric parts. Enumerate marker positions from the original input before applying any edits. Replay on each demo and compare the full output, including unchanged cells, overlap, clipping, and whether the source template persists. A visually plausible generic cross is not a substitute for the observed exact template.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-4/play/users/u012/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/template-stamp-marker-separation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/demo-output-mapping.md", "content": "# Validate the demo's output mapping before transfer

A visually structured demo output is not evidence by itself for reflection, tiling, or stamping. After the first plausible but incorrect submission, reopen the task class and output representation before changing coordinates. Record input/output dimensions, map input rows, columns, objects, or symbols to output cells/regions, and compare that candidate mapping against every available pair. Treat exact full-demo reproduction as a prerequisite for submitting; a coherent pattern that fails even one demo is still an unvalidated guess. If requesting another demo, identify which competing mappings its predicted output would distinguish.
