{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/session-f0caf098-output-representation.md", "content": "# Compact output representation and task-f0caf098 review

The demos repeatedly presented large regular lattices but returned compact outputs whose dimensions varied substantially. A key caution is that identifying separator crossings or extracting marker colors is only a candidate representation: neither the test's lattice dimensions nor a plausible crossing matrix establishes the output shape/content. Across demonstrations, explicitly align region/intersection positions to each compact output and test the precise encoding (including blank/default cells, omitted borders, ordering, and exceptional marker values). Require exact replay of every demo before submission.

In the reviewed instance, I made successive unsupported compact-grid guesses (including 4x4 and 5x5 crossing matrices) despite failed feedback. After any incorrect compact-output submission, restart representation inference from the demos and record which assumption each failure rejects; do not repeat the same candidate or cosmetically alter its entries. The available skill references already flag this output-dimension/mapping issue; this note captures the concrete failure pattern and emphasizes full representation validation before transfer.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-4/play/users/u012/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/session-f0caf098-output-representation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-stream-solving", "file": "references/expanded-output-mapping.md", "content": "# Expanded grids built from transformed input blocks

In some ARC tasks the output dimensions are an integer multiple of the input dimensions, and the output is a blockwise arrangement of transformed copies. Do not assume ordinary same-size editing or simple repetition. Partition the demo output into blocks matching the input's dimensions, then compare each block cell-by-cell with the input under rotations and reflections. Establish both the transform of each block and its position in the arrangement. Replay the complete mapping exactly on the demonstration before transferring it to a test input.

A 2x2 arrangement may use all four quarter-turn orientations, one per quadrant. Test orientation and block ordering independently; visually plausible patterns are insufficient. Once verified, construct each transformed block with explicit array operations and check the final dimensions and rows before submitting.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-4/play/users/u012/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/continual-arc-stream-solving/references/expanded-output-mapping.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/panel-count-encoding.md", "content": "# Panel marker-count encoding: diagnostic and validation lesson

This instance had grids partitioned by full separator bands and compact outputs shaped like the panel array. Several demos were consistent with an output marker for panels containing multiple instances of the designated non-background color; others had different marker colors and output backgrounds. A test answer based on a guessed threshold and manually counted panels was rejected, so the evidence does not establish the exact test encoding or make a reusable color/threshold rule.

Reusable method: transcribe the grid exactly; locate separator rows/columns; define half-open panel bounds; count target-color cells in every panel; build a table of panel coordinates, counts, and corresponding output values. Test competing count mappings (including exact count versus threshold) against *all* demonstrations, especially panels with zero, one, two, and three-or-more markers. Confirm orientation, output dimensions, background encoding, and values separately. After rejection, first independently re-check serialization and the panel count table; do not repeat a merely reformatted candidate. Do not claim a count rule until full demo replay supports it.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-4/play/users/u012/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/panel-count-encoding.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/failed-guess-reassessment.md", "content": "# Failed-submission reassessment

When a plausible full-grid cleanup or marker-repair guess is incorrect, treat that as evidence against the whole inferred rule, not a cue to make another nearby edit. Reconstruct the exact input/output delta in every demo, including unchanged markers and structures. Separate the observed operation from assumptions about color regions and object boundaries. If multiple rules still fit, name them and request a demo specifically predicted to differ; otherwise submit only a transformation replayed against all available examples. This avoids cascading unsupported guesses and needless penalties.
